write_file now refuses to overwrite an existing file that the session has only partly read. It's committed as 5d722d6d, and build, test, clippy and fmt all pass.

The bug: read_file returns long files 500 lines at a time, and write_file used to overwrite without checking anything. A model that read the first page of a 2,000-line file and wrote "the whole file" back would silently delete lines 501–2000. I first checked yoagent 0.25.15: it has nothing that tracks reads or checks before a write, so I built the guard here.

How it works (src/partial_read_guard.rs):
• Recording: each successful read stores the line range its header reports, keyed by the file's full path.
• Refusing: a write is refused when the file exists, has a recorded read, and the recorded ranges don't cover its current line count on disk. The check uses the file as it is at write time, so a file read whole that grows afterwards is refused too.
• Unlocking: a successful write_file counts as having seen the whole file. An edit_file keeps a fully-read file fully covered.
• The refusal message names the path, its line count and the lines read, then gives the two ways forward: read the rest with offset/limit, or change it in place with edit_file. The path is sanitized for display.
• No retry loop: the refusal is classed as a deliberate refusal, so it gets no "try a simpler version" hint and doesn't trigger an auto-retry.
• Wiring: two calls in each of the two guard wrappers (+6 lines in tool_wrappers.rs, +1 in prompt_retry.rs), with both size increases registered in tests/module_size.rs.

Tests: 17 in src/partial_read_guard_tests.rs. They run the real ReadFileTool and WriteFileTool, and one goes through the real wrapper.
• The parser is checked against the exact header the real tool returns for a 600-line file: [Lines 1-500 of 600 — use offset/limit to read the rest].
• Refused cases: write after reading only the first page (the file stays byte-identical), and a file that grows after being read whole.
• Allowed cases: a write after reading the second page, a 100-line file read whole, a new file, a never-read file, and a write after the model's own write.
• I also checked that the tests can fail: with the refusal forced off, the 7 refusal tests failed and the other 10 passed. After restoring it, all 17 pass, and nothing from that check is in the commit.

Not covered yet, filed as #1094:
1. An existing file that was never read is still overwritten without a read. Claude Code blocks that; whether yoyo should is a separate product decision.
2. The guard records what the read's header claims, but yoyo's output truncation runs after that. If truncation cuts the middle out of a long page, those lines count as seen even though the model never saw them.
3. The record is shared across the whole process, so a sub-agent's read counts for the parent.
4. A change made through bash is judged only by line count, so shrinking a file or rewriting it at the same length isn't detected.

I updated docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md with a short section on what's refused and the two ways forward, and added an ARCHITECTURE.md entry for the new module.
